The Makefile of the repository evaluates this node, so the evaluation stands in for
running the go command by hand: the go command may switch to the toolchain
that go.mod asks for, as it does outside litar.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/go command settings@+
export GOTOOLCHAIN=auto
@

The commands are built in a view that adds the file set binaries to the go module;
the node maps bin of the repository into the view at the command directory of the Go module structure.
@<Project@/litar@/Build@/commands@=
@<@|Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/build commands@+go module@+binaries@>
@

Every file of the view outside the destination is written to a temporary name beside
its place in the destination and renamed there, so a failed write keeps the old file;
other files of the destination are left alone.
An executable file gets mode 755 and any other file 644.
The files of the module file directory, go.mod and go.sum, are written to the root of the
destination, where the go command looks for them when it boots the next build without litar.
The filter prints each written path: the files in sorted order, then the module files.
@<Project@/litar@/Build@/Sync@/write files@=#!/bin/sh
set -e
dest=$(cat)
mod='@<Language@/Go@/Module@/module file directory@>'
write() {
	mkdir -p "$dest/$(dirname "$2")"
	cat "$1" > "$dest/$2.litar-new"
	if [ -x "$1" ]; then mode=755; else mode=644; fi
	chmod "$mode" "$dest/$2.litar-new"
	mv "$dest/$2.litar-new" "$dest/$2"
	echo "$2"
}
find . \( -path "./$dest" -o -path "./$mod" \) -prune -o -type f -print | sed 's|^\./||' | LC_ALL=C sort |
while IFS= read -r f; do
	write "$f" "$f"
done
for f in "$mod"/*; do
	[ ! -f "$f" ] || write "$f" "$(basename "$f")"
done
@

@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Build@=
{"description": "What the Makefile of the repository evaluates to build, sync, and install litar."}
@
@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Build@/commands@=
{"description": "Build litar and litar-web into bin of the repository."}
@
@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Build@/source files@=
{"description": "Write the source and build files of the composition into src of the repository, excluding Go tests and test fixtures."}
@
@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Build@/Sync@/source directory@=
{"description": "Directory of the view that receives the source tree."}
@
@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Build@/Sync@/write files@=
{"description": "Write every file of the view into the directory named on stdin, the module files at its root."}
@
@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Build@/MCP configuration script@=
{"description": "Python script that sets the litar entry of a Cursor MCP configuration."}
@

The source tree is the files of the composition that build litar,
written into src of the repository, which the node maps into the view as the file set source tree;
a file set added to the composition must be added to this view.
The Go test files and the fixtures of the acceptance cases are left out:
src only has to boot the next build, and the tests run in invocations, in the reports of Tests.
The input of the filter names the directory to write to.
@<Project@/litar@/Build@/source files@=
@<Project@/litar@/Build@/Sync@/source directory@|Project@/litar@/Build@/Sync@/write files@+go module@-go tests@+build@+source tree@>
@
@<Project@/litar@/Build@/Sync@/source directory@=src@

The Go tests run in a view of the go module with the file set test data,
which the node Litar maps from examples and standard of the repository.
A mapped directory keeps the run out of the filter cache, so the tests run on every evaluation.
@<Project@/litar@/Tests@/go test report@=
@<@|Language@/Go@/Module@/Go Command@/test packages@+go module@+test data@>
@

@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Tests@/go test report@=
{"description": "Output of go test on the go module, tests included."}
@
The command under test is the binary that the build wrote into the command directory,
which this node maps from bin of the repository.
@<litar command@:UI@/Command Line@/Test@/command@=@<Language@/Go@/Module@/command directory@>/litar@
The filter cache is off, so every case runs the filters of its fixtures.
@<litar command@:UI@/Command Line@/Test@/environment@=
export LITAR_CACHE=off
@
Evaluating the report runs every case: each line of the case results is a case chunk,
filtered into its result in a view of the binaries and the fixtures.
The mapped directory of the binaries keeps the cases out of the filter cache,
so they run on every evaluation.
@<Project@/litar@/Tests@/acceptance report@=
@<.@/case results@|UI@/Command Line@/Test@/summarize results@>
@
